ISSUE:
Changelog drops merge commits

Our release branches are merged with merge commits whose subjects carry the pull request titles, for example `Merge pull request #41 from team/export`, and the generated changelog never lists them.

`CommitsCmd("v1.2.0", "HEAD")` builds:

    git log --no-merges --pretty=format:%s v1.2.0..HEAD

Expected:

    git log --pretty=format:%s v1.2.0..HEAD

so merge subjects become part of the changelog.

Preferred fix: stop passing `--no-merges` when listing the commits for the changelog. Filtering a second query afterwards would also work, but it doubles the git calls.


A fix for this issue will edit the code below. It is the version BEFORE the fix.

FILE changelog/changelog.go (before the fix):
// Package changelog builds the git queries the changelog command runs and renders what they return.
package changelog

import (
	"fmt"
	"sort"
	"strings"
)

// logArgs is the git log prefix every history query shares.
func logArgs(format string) []string {
	return []string{"git", "log", "--no-merges", "--pretty=format:" + format}
}

// CommitsCmd lists the commit subjects between two refs.
func CommitsCmd(from, to string) []string {
	return append(logArgs("%s"), from+".."+to)
}

// AuthorsCmd lists the author of every commit between two refs, for the credits section.
func AuthorsCmd(from, to string) []string {
	return append(logArgs("%an <%ae>"), from+".."+to)
}

// LastTagCmd finds the most recent tag reachable from HEAD.
func LastTagCmd() []string {
	return []string{"git", "describe", "--tags", "--abbrev=0"}
}

// Render formats commit subjects as a markdown list under a version heading.
func Render(version string, subjects []string) string {
	var b strings.Builder
	fmt.Fprintf(&b, "## %s\n\n", version)
	for _, s := range subjects {
		s = strings.TrimSpace(s)
		if s == "" {
			continue
		}
		fmt.Fprintf(&b, "- %s\n", s)
	}
	return b.String()
}

// Contributors turns author lines into a sorted list without duplicates.
func Contributors(lines []string) []string {
	seen := map[string]bool{}
	var out []string
	for _, l := range lines {
		l = strings.TrimSpace(l)
		if l == "" || seen[l] {
			continue
		}
		seen[l] = true
		out = append(out, l)
	}
	sort.Strings(out)
	return out
}


EXISTING TEST changelog/changelog_test.go:
package changelog

import (
	"reflect"
	"strings"
	"testing"
)

func TestCommitsCmdRange(t *testing.T) {
	got := strings.Join(CommitsCmd("v1.0.0", "HEAD"), " ")
	if !strings.HasPrefix(got, "git log") || !strings.HasSuffix(got, "v1.0.0..HEAD") {
		t.Fatalf("CommitsCmd = %q", got)
	}
}

func TestLastTagCmd(t *testing.T) {
	want := []string{"git", "describe", "--tags", "--abbrev=0"}
	if got := LastTagCmd(); !reflect.DeepEqual(got, want) {
		t.Fatalf("LastTagCmd = %v", got)
	}
}

func TestRenderSkipsBlankSubjects(t *testing.T) {
	got := Render("v1.1.0", []string{"Add export", "  ", "Fix crash on empty config"})
	want := "## v1.1.0\n\n- Add export\n- Fix crash on empty config\n"
	if got != want {
		t.Fatalf("Render = %q", got)
	}
}

func TestContributorsDedupes(t *testing.T) {
	got := Contributors([]string{"bo <bo@x.io>", "al <al@x.io>", "bo <bo@x.io>"})
	want := []string{"al <al@x.io>", "bo <bo@x.io>"}
	if !reflect.DeepEqual(got, want) {
		t.Fatalf("Contributors = %v", got)
	}
}


Write probe cases that record what this code returns, so its output can be compared before and after the fix. Two kinds:
- "change": each input the issue says must behave differently, starting with its own examples. 2 to 3 cases.
- "keep": inputs that reach the same code but that the issue does NOT ask to change: the other functions and branches that share code with what the issue names, other options and flags, default, empty and boundary inputs, and the messages, commands and formatted strings the code builds. 6 to 10 cases. A correct fix leaves every keep output exactly as it was.

Output one Go code block: a file in package changelog, saved next to the existing test, so unexported identifiers and the existing test helpers are in scope. It declares func lokiCases() and no TestXxx function. Each case is one self-contained statement, with no setup shared between cases:
    lokiCase("k1", "keep", "<input in words>", func() any { return <call> })
Already defined, do not redeclare: lokiCase(id, kind, desc string, f func() any); lokiAll(vs ...any) any packs several return values, as in lokiAll(strconv.Atoi("7")); lokiStdout(f func()) string returns what f prints.
Return values; never assert, never call t.Fatal. Do not return pointers, times, random values, temp paths or channels. Import only what the cases use, and use only identifiers that exist in the code above.

